1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,450
...into the Indian Ocean to fish.

2
00:00:01,670 --> 00:00:03,570
At night. At three o'clock we start.

3
00:00:04,870 --> 00:00:07,400
The huge one weighed 50 to 55 kilos.

4
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:08,970
So really, like a person.

5
00:00:11,230 --> 00:00:14,330
Christians, Hindus, Muslims.

6
00:00:14,330 --> 00:00:16,130
It's a peaceful place.

7
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:18,400
The world, told by those who live it.

8
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:20,200
This is Voci Vere.

9
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:24,200
The day before, we're
on the beach in Marari:

10
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:26,800
boats pulled ashore,
nets spread out on tarps.

11
00:00:26,850 --> 00:00:29,000
Here, the work doesn't end
when you get back to shore.

12
00:00:41,250 --> 00:00:44,000
Imagine waking up at three in the morning,

13
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,700
by four you have to be heading
out into the Indian Ocean to fish,

14
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:49,670
and then bring the fish
to the market by eight.

15
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:52,170
He's getting the nets ready
for the next trip out to sea.

16
00:00:52,530 --> 00:00:54,170
Do you do this every day?

17
00:00:54,170 --> 00:00:56,970
Every day, fishing.

18
00:00:56,970 --> 00:00:58,770
Four o'clock. Four o'clock in the morning.

19
00:00:58,770 --> 00:01:00,670
Morning, eight o'clock.

20
00:01:00,670 --> 00:01:02,670
Sometimes, preparing.

21
00:01:02,670 --> 00:01:04,370
Can I see the net?

22
00:01:07,570 --> 00:01:10,100
This is to prepare to go fishing.

23
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:12,450
Incredible.

24
00:01:12,450 --> 00:01:14,700
You make it by hand?

25
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:15,900
Yes.

26
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:19,370
And you use it to go... to the ocean.

27
00:01:19,370 --> 00:01:21,870
Arabian. Arabian Sea.

28
00:01:21,870 --> 00:01:24,530
Ah, you call it the Arabian Sea. Yes.

29
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:27,200
A little further on, a fisherman
is sitting on his boat.

30
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:29,040
He's been doing this
work for thirty years.

31
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:31,350
Are you a fisherman?

32
00:01:31,350 --> 00:01:32,130
Yes, I'm a fisherman.

33
00:01:32,130 --> 00:01:34,430
No, no, at night.

34
00:01:34,430 --> 00:01:36,300
At night. At three o'clock we start.

35
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:40,350
At eight thirty, eight o'clock,
we come back with the fish.

36
00:01:40,550 --> 00:01:41,850
Then you go to the market?

37
00:01:41,850 --> 00:01:42,500
Market, market.

38
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:43,600
And for how many years?

39
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:45,400
Thirty years of fishing.

40
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:46,600
Three, zero. Yes.

41
00:01:46,850 --> 00:01:49,950
The day starts in the middle of the
night and ends when the fish is sold.

42
00:01:50,050 --> 00:01:51,710
But for him, it's not the only job.

43
00:01:52,070 --> 00:01:52,970
Kayaking.

44
00:01:53,170 --> 00:01:55,670
Ah, okay. You're a guide?

45
00:01:55,770 --> 00:01:56,930
Yes, a guide.

46
00:01:56,930 --> 00:01:59,130
And you like this more? Yes.

47
00:01:59,130 --> 00:02:00,780
One hour, two hours.

48
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:03,330
Narrow canals, very narrow.

49
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:07,090
Before we say goodbye, he wants to tell us
something about the place where he lives.

50
00:02:07,470 --> 00:02:10,370
How is life here in Kerala?

51
00:02:10,370 --> 00:02:13,300
Only fishing. It's good, it's good.

52
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:15,900
In Kerala, many religions.

53
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:19,000
Christians, Hindus, Muslims.

54
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,730
They get along very well. Friends.

55
00:02:22,730 --> 00:02:26,430
It's a peaceful place. A peaceful place.

56
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:27,850
And he tells us about the festival:

57
00:02:27,850 --> 00:02:31,480
two days without fishing, and
free food for anyone who comes.

58
00:02:31,830 --> 00:02:32,980
A thousand people come.

59
00:02:33,180 --> 00:02:36,130
There's free food for everyone.

60
00:02:36,380 --> 00:02:39,380
The next morning, we go to see
where all that work ends up.

61
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:44,730
Heading to the Marari fish market.

62
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,400
The host we're staying with is taking us.

63
00:02:47,700 --> 00:02:50,000
On the way back, we'll take a tuk-tuk.

64
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,400
The Mararikulam fish market isn't
a typical covered market building.

65
00:02:54,500 --> 00:03:00,200
It's a unique tradition of open-air
fish auctions, right on the beach.

66
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:05,400
Everyone waits... the boat arrives
with the fish and the auction starts.

67
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:26,300
The boats come in, and the fish passes from hand
to hand: into baskets, onto heads, onto the scales.

68
00:03:26,550 --> 00:03:28,770
I guess these are scales
to weigh the fish.

69
00:03:28,770 --> 00:03:30,370
The starting point for the bargaining.

70
00:03:30,370 --> 00:03:33,630
I'm trying not to be seen, but right
here there's a deal going on. Good.

71
00:03:35,230 --> 00:03:37,100
Looks like they've caught something...

72
00:03:37,150 --> 00:03:39,600
Let's see if I can show it.

73
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:43,200
Everyone's here to see
it, to try to buy it.

74
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:48,050
They just told me that
the fish from before,

75
00:03:48,050 --> 00:03:50,700
the huge one, weighed 50 to 55 kilos.

76
00:03:50,950 --> 00:03:52,470
So really, like a person.

77
00:03:52,470 --> 00:03:57,000
This is the fish market in
Mararikulam. It's very famous.

78
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:00,270
And every day, in the morning,
they do the same thing.

79
00:04:00,270 --> 00:04:01,670
A long time.

80
00:04:01,670 --> 00:04:03,270
A long time ago. Yes, yes.

81
00:04:03,270 --> 00:04:07,100
The guy who brought us
here said that, of course,

82
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:11,400
this is one of the main jobs people
do here, where we are, in Marari,

83
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:13,800
but also all over Kerala.

84
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:16,800
You know, this giant fish
must be worth a lot of rupees.

85
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:21,200
He also explained that there are bigger
boats that stay out for as long as two days

86
00:04:21,200 --> 00:04:24,650
in the Indian Ocean. Here
they call it the Arabian Sea.

87
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:27,750
And then there are these
other, smaller boats,

88
00:04:27,750 --> 00:04:29,100
that go out and come back every day,

89
00:04:29,100 --> 00:04:31,000
like the fishermen from
yesterday were telling us:

90
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:34,400
they get up at three, go fishing at
four, and by eight they're back here.

91
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:36,470
The other kind stays out
at sea for several days.

92
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:46,400
This one's been sold.

93
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:49,800
Ice for the fish.

94
00:04:50,050 --> 00:04:52,780
Everything moves fast: the fish
has to leave while it's fresh.

95
00:05:01,250 --> 00:05:02,670
And of course, people eat here.

96
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:06,200
This is Kerala's pizza, in India: parotta.

97
00:05:12,950 --> 00:05:15,630
And this is Indian chai, made fresh.

98
00:05:21,150 --> 00:05:24,500
Then you tear it with your hands...

99
00:05:26,900 --> 00:05:28,970
A little kitten waits
for the leftover fish.

100
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:33,700
For the people who work here,
it's a morning like any other.

101
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:36,590
For us, the most direct way
to understand this coast.

102
00:05:42,450 --> 00:05:46,350
The nets that were spread on the sand
yesterday were out at sea last night.

103
00:05:46,450 --> 00:05:48,370
And at three in the
morning, it starts again.

104
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:52,260
We didn't come to explain this
life. We came to listen to it.

105
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:56,700
The Voci Vere journey, giving a voice
to those who need it most, continues.

106
00:05:57,200 --> 00:06:00,200
Follow me to discover the next story.

